lization), the dimmer opacity is set during finalize: QQuickPopupPrivate::finalizeVisible → setDim(dim/dimmer opacity = popup->dim()). QQuickDrawer… hmm dim() defaults to 1. But Drawer overrides opacity in setPosition; if it's never called the dim is… The screenshot doesn't show a dark overlay so either dim opacity is 0 or the dim doesn't exist.\n\nOK. Decision: the correct fix is:\n\n1. Remove `visible: view.count > 1` from the Drawer (let open()/close() manage visibility). Keep the drawer interactive so swipe-up from the bottom edge opens it regardless of page count (needed for first run setup anyway).\n\n2. `modal` — Drawer defaults to modal:true. The current usage relies on `bottomBarSettings.close()` when clicking items and CloseOnReleaseOutside. Modal drawer dims the background. Currently when the drawer opens (screenshot 2) there's no visible dim… because dim opacity = position? Screenshot 2 shows no dim. If we make it modal:false, it won't block input and there's no dim either; the explicit close() calls handle closing. And CloseOnReleaseOutside (closePolicy) closes it when tapping outside even when non-modal. The current behavior works; changing modal is not requested — keep it? Hmm, but \"la barre doit être affichée au bord bas\" — no problem.\n\n3. Also, to stabilize the \"position verticale\" issue on desktop resize: the weird state came from a closed-but-visible drawer. After fix #1, that state no longer occurs. Additionally, a closed drawer is not visible at all on resize. Should be sufficient.\n\nBut wait — if we remove the visible binding: when visible becomes false after close(), the Popup closes — good. Does an edge swipe still work to open it again? From qquickdrawer.cpp: `startDrag` — it's called from QQuickPopup::startDrag which is called by… Let me recall where QQuickPopup::startDrag is called from: from QQuickOverlayPrivate::handleHover… actually in qquickoverlay.cpp: the overlay iterates through `QQuickPopup`s to call `popup->startDrag(event)